<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kikuyu people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gikuyu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kikuyu are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> people of central Kenya, living around Mount Kenya, and the largest ethnic group in the country. Under British rule they lost much land to European settlers in the highlands, and Kikuyu associations led early African political protest. They were central to the Mau Mau rising of the 1950s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jomo-kenyatta">Jomo Kenyatta</a>, Kenya's first president, was Kikuyu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kikuyu-people.jpg" alt="Kikuyu people" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fotograaf / photographer: H.W. (Henk) van Rinsum (Waingapoe 1932) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COLLECTIE_TROPENMUSEUM_Een_dansvoorstelling_van_de_Village_Dancers_TMnr_20038859.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
